WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.110
In this film you look inside the regulatory part of a FundsXML file: an EMT block for distributors, and a TPT block for insurers.

2
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.990
It takes about five minutes.

3
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.226
All regulatory data sits in one block at the top level: RegulatoryReportings.

4
00:00:17.276 --> 00:00:19.057
It splits into two branches.

5
00:00:19.107 --> 00:00:25.532
Direct reporting holds templates that describe the fund or share class itself, such as EMT and EET.

6
00:00:25.582 --> 00:00:33.118
Indirect reporting holds data that others use for their own reporting, such as the TPT for insurers and PRIIPs data.

7
00:00:33.168 --> 00:00:36.192
Each template version is an element of its own.

8
00:00:37.143 --> 00:00:40.419
Here it is in the file, after the asset master data.

9
00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:49.502
Regulatory reportings, then direct reporting, and inside it the element EMT V four two: the European MiFID Template in version four point two.

10
00:00:49.552 --> 00:00:58.521
This example file is written for FundsXML four point two point nine; newer schema versions add EMT four point three as an element of its own.

11
00:00:59.173 --> 00:01:06.308
An EMT block describes one financial instrument, here the euro retail class; the file has one for each share class.

12
00:01:06.358 --> 00:01:11.993
Data set information names the template version, the producer and which parts are reported.

13
00:01:12.043 --> 00:01:19.553
General information identifies the share class by its ISIN, and gives its name, its currency, and whether it distributes.

14
00:01:20.205 --> 00:01:35.898
The target market follows the MiFID categories: which investor types the fund is meant for, their knowledge and experience, their ability to bear losses, the risk indicator, here three, and the client objectives: capital growth, with a recommended holding period of five years.

15
00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:45.852
And the ex-ante costs: ongoing costs, management fee, transaction costs and incidental costs, given as decimal fractions of the net asset value.

16
00:01:45.902 --> 00:01:50.139
Zero point zero one five eight means one point five eight percent.

17
00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:55.285
Below, in indirect reporting, the Tripartite Template in version seven.

18
00:01:55.335 --> 00:02:05.912
At portfolio level: the TPT version, the identifier of the share class, its name and currency, the fund's total net assets, the valuation date and the reporting date.

19
00:02:06.563 --> 00:02:07.666
Then the positions.

20
00:02:07.716 --> 00:02:16.533
Each one starts with its CIC code, the Complementary Identification Code: four characters for the country of quotation and the kind of instrument.

21
00:02:16.583 --> 00:02:23.578
U S three one is a listed equity quoted in the United States, here the same fictional share we met in the portfolio.

22
00:02:23.628 --> 00:02:31.474
Then the instrument code and name, and the valuation: quantity, market values in quotation and portfolio currency, and the weight.

23
00:02:31.524 --> 00:02:35.786
Credit risk data names the issuer, its country and its economic sector.

24
00:02:36.437 --> 00:02:38.453
Further down is a government bond.

25
00:02:38.503 --> 00:02:42.405
Its CIC code is D E one one: a government bond quoted in Germany.

26
00:02:42.455 --> 00:02:50.489
For bonds, the template adds the interest rate data: rate type, coupon rate and frequency, and the redemption with its maturity date.

27
00:02:50.539 --> 00:02:57.906
Together with the modified duration below, that is the information an insurer needs for its interest rate and spread risk.

28
00:02:59.056 --> 00:03:01.322
The other templates work the same way.

29
00:03:01.372 --> 00:03:07.355
The EET for sustainability data and the EFT for feedback from distributors sit in direct reporting.

30
00:03:07.405 --> 00:03:10.646
The PRIIPs data of the EPT sits in indirect reporting.

31
00:03:10.696 --> 00:03:21.129
Each has its own element per version, and older versions remain in the schema for a transition period, so sender and receiver can move to a new version when both are ready.

32
00:03:22.479 --> 00:03:27.412
Templates follow their own conventions, and they differ from the core of FundsXML.

33
00:03:27.462 --> 00:03:29.478
Flags are single letters, Y and N.

34
00:03:29.528 --> 00:03:34.461
Percentages are often decimal fractions, where one stands for one hundred percent.

35
00:03:34.511 --> 00:03:41.874
And many fields are numbered codes from the template's own lists, for example code one for an ISIN as the identifier type.

36
00:03:41.924 --> 00:03:47.708
Read the template's documentation for each field, and do not assume the conventions of the core.

37
00:03:49.058 --> 00:03:50.717
One thing is worth knowing.

38
00:03:50.767 --> 00:03:57.742
Much of what the templates ask for also exists in the core of FundsXML: identifiers, values, issuers and dates.

39
00:03:57.792 --> 00:04:05.401
The core describes funds and assets neutrally; a template block is a view of that data in the form a regulation asks for.

40
00:04:05.451 --> 00:04:11.224
Converters can fill a template from the core data, so the same fact is not maintained twice.

41
00:04:12.574 --> 00:04:13.882
And when you need more?

42
00:04:13.932 --> 00:04:21.263
Country-specific data carries national reporting at the top level and in several blocks, for example for Austria and Germany.

43
00:04:21.313 --> 00:04:27.109
Custom attributes let partners exchange extra fields in a defined way, without breaking the schema.

44
00:04:27.159 --> 00:04:30.003
Use them sparingly, and agree on them in writing.

45
00:04:31.353 --> 00:04:34.482
That was the regulatory part of a FundsXML file.

46
00:04:34.532 --> 00:04:39.053
In the next film, you validate a delivery and check its data quality.

47
00:04:39.103 --> 00:04:40.378
Thanks for watching.
